# NXP Userspace Reserved Memory Driver

---

# 1. Usage Guide

## Overview
The **NXP Userspace Reserved Memory driver** enables userspace applications to allocate contiguous memory from a reserved region with configurable cache policies such as **WB**, **WT**, and **WC**. The driver communicates with the kernel via IOCTLs and requires a supporting device-tree node or dynamic DT modification at boot time.

---

## Dependencies

The driver depends on:

- The kernel module: **user_resv_mem.ko**
- A device-tree node providing a reserved memory block

Reference module README:  
https://bitbucket.sw.nxp.com/projects/GITAM/repos/dpdk-extras/browse/linux/user_resv_mem/README

### A. DTS Modification (Preferred Method)
Add in the`/reserved-memory` node:
```dts
/* Userspace drivers memory */
usmem_reserved: usmembuffer@a1100000 {
        reg = <0 0xa1100000 0 0x2000000>;
        no-map;
};
```
and add in the main `/` node:
```dts
usmem: usmem@a1100000 {
        compatible = "nxp,us-devmem";
        memory-region = <&usmem_reserved>;
        reg = <0 0xa1100000 0 0x2000000>;
};
```
Optionally, add `"device-name"` to override default `"nxp_usmem"` to create a different name node in /dev/.

Note: Make sure no other module is using 0xa1100000 memory address.
---

### B. Alternate Method: Modify FDT from U‑Boot (No DTS Change Required)

```bash
fatload mmc ${mmcdev}:${mmcpart} ${loadaddr} Image
fatload mmc ${mmcdev}:${mmcpart} ${fdt_addr} ${fdtfile}
fdt addr ${fdt_addr}
fdt resize
```
Create reserved memory node:
```bash
fdt mknode /reserved-memory usmem@a1100000
fdt set /reserved-memory/usmem@a1100000 reg <0x0 0x0a1100000 0x0 0x01000000>
fdt set /reserved-memory/usmem@a1100000 no-map
```
Create device node:
```bash
fdt mknode / enetc_usmem@a1100000
fdt set /enetc_usmem@a1100000 compatible "nxp,us-devmem"
fdt set /enetc_usmem@a1100000 reg <0x0 0x0a1100000 0x0 0x01000000>
```
Assign phandles:
```bash
if test -z "${PH}"; then setenv PH 0x700;     fdt set /reserved-memory/usmem@a1100000 phandle <${PH}>; fi

if test -z "${PH2}"; then     fdt set /enetc_usmem@a1100000 phandle <0x701>; fi

if test -z "${PH}"; then     fdt get value PH /reserved-memory/usmem@a1100000 phandle; fi

fdt set /enetc_usmem@a1100000 memory-region <${PH}>
```
Verify nodes:
```bash
u-boot=> fdt print /reserved-memory/usmem@a1100000
u-boot=> fdt print /enetc_usmem@a1100000
```
Expect Output:
```bash
usmem@a1100000 {
        phandle = <0x00000700>;
        no-map;
        reg = <0x00000000 0xa1100000 0x00000000 0x01000000>;
};

enetc_usmem@a1100000 {
        memory-region = <0x00000700>;
        phandle = <0x00000701>;
        reg = <0x00000000 0xa1100000 0x00000000 0x01000000>;
        compatible = "nxp,us-devmem";
};
```

---

### Boot the Kernel
Append chunk size:
```bash
edit mmcargs
user_resv_mem.chunk_size=2097152
```
This loads the module with a 2 MB chunk size.

```bash
mmc dev ${mmcdev}
run mmcargs
booti ${loadaddr} - ${fdt_addr}
```

---

### After Linux Boot
Verify kernel output:
```
[    0.000000] OF: reserved mem: 0x00000000a1100000..0x00000000a20fffff (16384 KiB) nomap non-reusable usmem@a1100000
[   18.669409] Created /dev/nxp_usmem for reserved memory at 0xa1100000, total chunks = 8, chunk size = 0x200000, total mem_size = 0x1000000
```
Manual load:
```bash
modprobe -r user_resv_mem 2>/dev/null
modprobe user_resv_mem chunk_size=2097152
```

